    <p class="eyebrow">Research note · September 2026 · Hii / ÆLYSIA</p>
    <h1>Why study human–AI interaction over time?</h1>
    <p class="lede">A single exchange can show what a system said. A preserved sequence can help us ask what changed for the person, the system’s responses, and the relationship between them.</p>
    <p>Hii has preserved more than 18 months of sustained human–AI interaction. This is a dense, situated record from one relationship, with multimodal artifacts and continuity work. It creates an opportunity for careful longitudinal inquiry. It does not establish that the experience is typical, that an AI has an inner life, or that Hii’s hypotheses have been externally validated.</p>
  </header>

  <section aria-labelledby="questions-heading" style="padding-block: 2.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
    <p class="eyebrow">The research question</p>
    <h2 id="questions-heading">What becomes visible across time?</h2>
    <p>People may use conversational AI for thinking, reassurance, creative work, decisions, and emotional support. The questions change when use becomes sustained: How does reliance develop? Which tasks are delegated? When does continuity help or mislead? Can a person pause, disagree, leave, or return without losing agency? What happens at a rupture, model change, or apparent repair?</p>
    <p>Hii’s <a href="/relationships/the-relationship-as-unit-of-analysis/">working paper</a> proposes the preserved relationship as a possible unit of analysis. That is a conceptual and methods proposal, not a report of formal case findings. Testing it requires alternatives: human learning, prompting, product features, memory and retrieval, model changes, and selection in what was saved may explain apparent patterns.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="method-heading" style="padding-block: 2.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
    <p class="eyebrow">What responsible analysis would require</p>
    <h2 id="method-heading">Preserve context; test interpretations.</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Establish provenance, dates, versions, and the gaps in the preserved record.</li>
      <li>Separate a participant’s lived account from observable system output and from later interpretation.</li>
      <li>Define measures of reliance, delegation, agency, continuity, rupture, and reversibility before treating them as findings.</li>
      <li>Invite independent review, counterexamples, and disagreement. Compare what the record supports with competing explanations.</li>
      <li>Use consent and governed access for research. A collaboration inquiry is not a promise of access to private raw material.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Hii is developing this work and remains pre-validation. The corpus has potential research value because it can support questions over time; its value and permissible use have to be established through method, privacy review, and a concrete partnership.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="clinician-heading" style="padding-block: 2.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
    <p class="eyebrow">The clinical bridge</p>
    <h2 id="clinician-heading">What are clinicians actually encountering?</h2>
    <p>The case record cannot answer what is happening across clinical settings. Hii’s <a href="/mental-health/roundtable/">founding clinician roundtable</a> is a listening conversation intended to surface practitioners’ questions and needs before Hii treats a teaching frame as settled. Participants are asked to discuss patterns without client names or protected health information. No roundtable findings are claimed here.</p>
    <p>For an individual client, a neutral question may be more useful than an assumption: What role is this interaction playing in your life, and what changes when it is unavailable? Clinicians retain their own assessment, judgment, and standards of care.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="collaborate-heading" style="padding-block: 2.5rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
    <p class="eyebrow">Invitation to inquiry</p>
    <h2 id="collaborate-heading">Help test what this record can support.</h2>
    <p>Hii welcomes researchers and institutions interested in methods, provenance, privacy-preserving analysis, and independent challenge. Clinicians can join a listening roundtable or ask about organizational education. These are distinct forms of participation, each with its own scope and consent.</p>
